<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    V8 allocates its code pages with MAP_JIT, and this is what authorizes that
    under the hardened runtime. This binary JITs in two roles: Electron's main
    process runs Node and V8 directly, and the harness is this same executable
    re-launched as Node (shell/src/host-process.cjs spawns process.execPath with
    ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE), so it is governed by this file too.

    Four entitlements were dropped here after an audit of the shipped 0.1.24
    bundle. Each removal, and what it rested on:

      allow-unsigned-executable-memory — V8 needs MAP_JIT, not plain RWX.
        Chrome's universal renderer and GPU helpers on this machine ship
        allow-jit alone, x86_64 slice included. koffi's own shipped docs
        (node_modules/koffi/doc/callbacks.md) state it uses static trampolines
        and generates no code at runtime, and the only koffi.load() in the tree
        loads kernel32.dll on Windows.

      disable-library-validation — build/afterPack.cjs stages the harness
        closure BEFORE electron-builder signs, so every native artifact in
        Contents/Resources/harness is re-signed with this app's identity. Ten
        were checked individually (sharp, libvips, koffi, cua-driver's runtime
        and dylib, rg, pty.node, spawn-helper and the rest) and all reported
        the app signing team. Library validation accepts same-team and Apple code,
        which covers all of it. spawn-helper is exec'd as its own process and
        links only libSystem and libc++, so the parent's validation state never
        applies to it.

      allow-dyld-environment-variables — ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE is not a dynamic
        linker variable and the hardened runtime does not filter it. Nothing in
        this repo sets any DYLD_ variable; host-process.cjs strips the whole
        DYLD_ prefix from the harness child. Keeping this re-permitted
        DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES against a hardened binary, which is the opposite
        of what that code is for.

      com.apple.security.inherit — an App Sandbox entitlement, inert without
        com.apple.security.app-sandbox, which this Developer ID build does not
        set. Its comment claimed spawned shells "inherit the sandbox decision";
        there was no sandbox decision to inherit. Apple also places this key on
        nested helpers, never on a main bundle.

    The one thing to watch: if the harness ever loads a native addon from a
    user's project folder rather than from inside this bundle, library
    validation will refuse it. Restore disable-library-validation HERE only,
    not in the inherit file.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
